// CLAIM: Publishing a component library needs a compiler newer than any release
// HOLDS: by-inspection
// WHAT-WOULD-CHECK-IT: nothing this suite can run, because **the claim is about the machine rather
//      than about star**. It holds on a `burxt` without `burxt where` and stops holding on one with it,
//      so a probe would assert a property of whatever compiler happens to be on `PATH` — green here and
//      red on the next machine, or the reverse, with neither answer saying anything about this
//      repository. A gate whose result depends on the environment reports the environment.
//
//      What IS checkable, and was checked rather than assumed on 2026-08-19, is both sides of the fork:
//
//        1. With a compiler that has `where`: a package component resolved, generated to
//           `.star/mylib/Card.bx` in the CONSUMER's tree, imported as `use ".star/mylib/Card.bx"`, and
//           `star-check` reported `no errors` / `no problems` through all three layers. Props were
//           checked at the call site across the package boundary — a missing one gave STAR-E017 naming
//           the component and its props, exactly as a local component does.
//        2. With the compiler that is installed: `cannot read mylib/Card.sbmx, imported by Uses.sbmx`,
//           and underneath it Burxt's own message, passed through rather than reworded.
//
//      The whole suite is green on the installed compiler, which is the assertion that matters most
//      here: **the feature costs nothing to a document that does not use it.** `burxt where` is asked
//      first for every `.sbmx` import, and a plain path import simply falls through to what it always
//      did.
// WHY: recorded rather than skipped, so the escape hatch costs an argument rather than a shrug.
//
//      This claim replaced one that said a component could not come from a package at all. That was
//      true when it was written this morning and false by the afternoon, which is the failure mode
//      `tools/limits.bx` exists for — **a stale NOT-DONE is found by nobody.** It survived the gate
//      because a by-inspection note is prose and the gate cannot read prose. Worth knowing when
//      reaching for `.note` again: it buys an honest record and buys no protection at all.
